WEBVTT

00:00:00.273 --> 00:00:02.800
Use the property the
evidence actually shows

00:00:03.174 --> 00:00:07.396
A microphone converts sound into an
electrical signal, and an oscilloscope

00:00:07.431 --> 00:00:09.181
displays voltage against time.

00:00:09.591 --> 00:00:12.964
Is that wavy line the actual
path followed by air particles?

00:00:13.775 --> 00:00:14.080
No.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.422
It is a graph of the
microphone signal over time.

00:00:18.413 --> 00:00:21.683
The air sound wave is longitudinal
even if the screen line

00:00:21.687 --> 00:00:23.261
looks like a transverse wave.

00:00:24.072 --> 00:00:24.738
Excellent.

00:00:25.142 --> 00:00:27.601
Compare traces only after
checking the settings.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:31.687
With the same gain, a larger signal
amplitude generally indicates a

00:00:31.687 --> 00:00:33.275
stronger sound at the microphone.

00:00:33.711 --> 00:00:38.296
With the same timescale, more cycles
across the screen means higher frequency.

00:00:39.049 --> 00:00:42.859
If someone changes the oscilloscope's
gain or time base, apparent

00:00:42.864 --> 00:00:45.881
height or spacing might change
without the sound changing.

00:00:46.685 --> 00:00:47.417
Precisely.

00:00:47.831 --> 00:00:51.921
Horizontal separation between equivalent
points on a time trace is the period,

00:00:52.231 --> 00:00:54.316
the time for one complete cycle.

00:00:54.781 --> 00:00:55.913
It is not wavelength.

00:00:56.154 --> 00:00:58.599
Wavelength is a distance
measured in space.

00:00:59.386 --> 00:01:02.936
So a trace with four cycles in
one-hundredth of a second has

00:01:02.938 --> 00:01:08.050
frequency four divided by zero
point zero one, four hundred hertz.

00:01:08.686 --> 00:01:09.218
Correct.

00:01:09.471 --> 00:01:13.069
Its period is zero point
zero zero two five seconds.

00:01:13.401 --> 00:01:15.519
Frequency and period are reciprocals.

00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:19.544
Frequency equals one divided
by period, and period equals

00:01:19.547 --> 00:01:21.154
one divided by frequency.

00:01:21.898 --> 00:01:23.063
Milliseconds.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:28.085
I must convert them into seconds before
using a formula that expects seconds.

00:01:28.541 --> 00:01:31.648
Two point five milliseconds
is not two point five seconds.

00:01:32.431 --> 00:01:33.272
Exactly.

00:01:33.712 --> 00:01:37.659
A thousand-fold error would make this
teacher drop her imaginary microphone.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:42.606
Check the axis units before calculating,
and count complete cycles rather than

00:01:42.618 --> 00:01:44.525
individual peaks without intervals.

00:01:44.956 --> 00:01:47.583
Why do we see lightning
before hearing thunder?

00:01:48.405 --> 00:01:50.796
Light travels vastly faster than sound.

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:54.089
Sound in ordinary air is
roughly three hundred and forty

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:57.320
meters per second, depending on
conditions such as temperature.

00:01:58.098 --> 00:01:58.493
Good.

00:01:58.849 --> 00:02:02.142
Representative speeds are about
fifteen hundred meters per second

00:02:02.142 --> 00:02:04.785
in water and five thousand in steel.

00:02:05.174 --> 00:02:09.399
Many solids carry sound faster than
liquids or gases, but this is not a

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:11.524
universal ranking for every material.

00:02:12.323 --> 00:02:15.274
Is the only explanation that
particles are closer together?

00:02:16.031 --> 00:02:16.403
No.

00:02:16.939 --> 00:02:21.568
Both stiffness and density matter, and
solids can carry different wave types.

00:02:21.966 --> 00:02:24.790
Particle spacing alone is
not enough to predict speed.

00:02:25.142 --> 00:02:28.389
For our examples, the material's
elastic response lets the

00:02:28.389 --> 00:02:29.955
disturbance travel faster.

00:02:30.719 --> 00:02:33.814
Wavelength is the distance between
equivalent neighboring points,

00:02:34.017 --> 00:02:35.780
such as consecutive compressions.

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:40.696
The wave equation is speed equals
frequency times wavelength.

00:02:41.177 --> 00:02:45.284
At unchanged speed, what happens to
wavelength when frequency doubles?

00:02:45.976 --> 00:02:46.783
It halves.

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:51.922
A higher frequency does not automatically
mean sound travels faster through

00:02:51.923 --> 00:02:53.918
the same ordinary air conditions.

00:02:54.655 --> 00:02:55.249
Correct.

00:02:55.644 --> 00:02:59.963
Try a sound traveling at three hundred and
forty meters per second with a frequency

00:02:59.969 --> 00:03:01.865
of one hundred and seventy hertz.

00:03:02.226 --> 00:03:03.435
Find its wavelength.

00:03:04.296 --> 00:03:08.409
Speed divided by frequency, three
hundred and forty divided by one

00:03:08.409 --> 00:03:10.455
hundred and seventy gives two meters.

00:03:10.842 --> 00:03:13.751
I include meters because
wavelength is a distance.

00:03:14.458 --> 00:03:17.846
A three-second flash to thunder
delay gives a rough distance of

00:03:17.846 --> 00:03:21.617
one kilometer, but never stay
outside to collect storm timings.

00:03:22.138 --> 00:03:25.721
Hearing thunder is a reason to
seek proper shelter, not start

00:03:25.722 --> 00:03:27.310
a science challenge outdoors.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.459
The estimate is physics
practice, not a safety guarantee.

00:03:32.027 --> 00:03:36.479
A complicated storm also is not a
neat single source at one exact point

00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:39.263
Trace the sound's journey through your ear

00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:42.746
The pinna helps collect
sound into the ear canal.

00:03:43.147 --> 00:03:45.130
Sound makes the eardrum vibrate.

00:03:45.526 --> 00:03:49.874
Tiny middle ear bones transfer the
vibration towards the fluid-filled cochlea

00:03:50.673 --> 00:03:51.444
Exactly.

00:03:51.737 --> 00:03:55.467
Sensitive cells in the cochlea
respond, producing signals carried

00:03:55.467 --> 00:03:57.321
along the auditory nerve to the brain.

00:03:57.764 --> 00:04:01.067
You experience sound through
that whole system, not just

00:04:01.068 --> 00:04:02.465
through the visible outer ear

00:04:03.274 --> 00:04:08.125
And loud sounds can damage those delicate
structures, sometimes permanently

00:04:08.754 --> 00:04:09.334
Yes.

00:04:09.725 --> 00:04:12.801
Risk depends on sound
level and exposure time.

00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:16.902
Keep listening levels comfortable,
take breaks, and use suitable

00:04:16.902 --> 00:04:18.888
hearing protection in noisy settings.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:22.492
Do not turn up audio to test
the limit of your hearing

00:04:23.209 --> 00:04:26.985
The often quoted human range is
about twenty hertz to twenty thousand

00:04:26.994 --> 00:04:31.262
hertz, but people vary, and the
upper limit commonly falls with age

00:04:31.996 --> 00:04:32.562
Correct.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:35.004
Infrasound is below twenty hertz.

00:04:35.221 --> 00:04:37.442
Ultrasound is above twenty thousand.

00:04:37.836 --> 00:04:40.000
They are still mechanical sound
waves, even though those frequencies
